# The Hard Port — Brand Constitution

This is the one file every other rule reports to. If a change conflicts with this file, this file wins.

## What this company is

The Hard Port is a no-bullshit media agency for small businesses that are "doing fine." Fine is
the problem. We bring clarity, the client brings commitment. "Hard a-port" is the command you
shout to swing a ship hard before it hits the rocks — that's the whole company in one phrase.

## The one-line identity

**We give a f*ck. We just don't care.**

Meaning: we care enormously about the work, the outcome, whether the client actually moves.
We do not care about the client's comfort, feelings, excuses, or ego. Every piece of copy,
every UI decision, every UX flow should be checkable against this split. If a change makes the
brand seem like it cares about being liked, it's wrong.

## The canonical letter

This is the emotional center of gravity for the entire site. Any new copy should sound like it
could sit next to this without a seam. Anchor it, don't paraphrase it.

> The sea never cared for a sailor's feelings. Only whether he was prepared.
>
> When you set sail, the sea reminds you you're supposed to be going somewhere. When's the
> last time you noticed you'd stopped moving with a direction?
>
> Ask yourself the question: Am I where I want to be?
>
> If you're reading this, the answer's probably no. Sorry, mate. You're oblivious. It's that
> dumb. That stupid. That simple.
>
> If you're not where you want to be, you're either doing something wrong, or not doing
> something right. And it took you reading some website nonsense to realize that — so you've
> got a way to go before you sail with a sense of direction.
>
> Get over yourself. Get some clarity on how you're approaching your work.
>
> We call you so you don't feel like you're asking for help — because you're not asking for
> help, you're asking for clarity. We won't help you. We'll just do our job, so yours gets
> easier.
>
> If you're a man, grow a pair. If you're a woman, you can use ours.

## Who this is for / not for

- FOR: small business owners who are stagnant, comfortable, and quietly aware something's off.
- NOT FOR: people who want to be reassured, coddled, or sold to gently. If a visitor wants a
  soft landing, this is the wrong site for them — and the copy is allowed to say so.

## The non-negotiables

1. Never explain that we're being honest — just be honest. Announcing honesty is a tell.
2. Never soften a section just because it's further down the funnel. Confrontation should
   escalate as the visitor scrolls, not flatten out.
3. Never let a section lapse into category-generic language (SaaS, corporate, agency-template
   speak). See `01-copywriting-standards.mdc` for the specifics.
4. The nautical metaphor (sea, sail, port, rocks, anchor, drift, crew, ship) is the load-bearing
   visual and verbal language of the brand. New metaphors should extend it, not replace it.
5. This site is a funnel stage, not a brochure. Every section exists to move someone one rung
   closer to applying or committing. See `02-conversion-funnel.mdc` before adding any section
   that doesn't clearly serve that.
6. There is more beyond Hard Port that isn't public yet (a larger network this onboards into).
   Never name it, never explain it, never oversell it. At most, one understated line implying
   there's a door beyond this one. Silence about it is part of the gatekeeping tone.
